Do not dispatch fake mouse moves while the page is driven by touch

LocalFrameView::scheduleScrollEvent() (Source/WebCore/page/LocalFrameView.cpp:6393-6398)
calls EventHandler::dispatchFakeMouseMoveEventSoon() on every scroll position change.
dispatchFakeMouseMoveEventSoon() (Source/WebCore/page/EventHandler.cpp:3967-3990) arms a
100 ms / 250 ms one-shot timer that is restarted on every scroll, so it fires shortly after
each drag stops. When it fires it runs Document::hitTest -- which calls Document::updateLayout()
unconditionally -- and then Document::updateHoverActiveState, which rebuilds the hover/active
chains and can invalidate style with Style::InvalidationScope::Descendants on an ancestor.

The point of that work is to refresh :hover under a pointer that did not move while the content
scrolled out from under it. On a touch-only device there is no pointer, so all of it is waste.

Measured with uprobes on a Pixel 2 XL (aarch64, GTK4/Wayland, Epiphany), one 11.7 s continuous
finger drag of a settled 74 000 px Wikipedia article:

    EventHandler::dispatchFakeMouseMoveEventSoon   314 calls   (one per scroll position change)
    Document::hitTest(request, location, result)    75 calls,   97 ms
    Document::updateHoverActiveState                73 calls,  437 ms   (max 25 ms)

That is over half a second of main-thread time in a 11.7 s drag, spent on hover state for a
pointer that does not exist, and each of those 75 hit tests also forces a layout.

Approach

ChromeClient::shouldDispatchFakeMouseMoveEvents() (Source/WebCore/page/ChromeClient.h:651,
default true) already gates dispatchFakeMouseMoveEventSoon() at EventHandler.cpp:3978, and it is
that call site's only consumer. The WebKit2 plumbing behind it is complete and unused:

    WebChromeClient::shouldDispatchFakeMouseMoveEvents()
        Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebCoreSupport/WebChromeClient.cpp:1875-1878
    WebPage::shouldDispatchFakeMouseMoveEvents() / setShouldDispatchFakeMouseMoveEvents()
        Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebPage/WebPage.h:1601, :2522, backing m_shouldDispatchFakeMouseMoveEvents at :3081
    IPC message SetShouldDispatchFakeMouseMoveEvents
        Source/WebKit/WebProcess/WebPage/WebPage.messages.in:653
    WebPageProxy::setShouldDispatchFakeMouseMoveEvents(bool)
        Source/WebKit/UIProcess/WebPageProxy.h:2097 (public), Source/WebKit/UIProcess/WebPageProxy.cpp:15062

A tree-wide grep for setShouldDispatchFakeMouseMoveEvents / SetShouldDispatchFakeMouseMoveEvents
finds only the declaration, the definition, the message and the WebPage setter: no port anywhere
in the tree calls the WebPageProxy setter. This patch is its first caller.

So this is a UI-process, port-local change: WebKitWebViewBase tracks whether the last input came
from touch and flips the existing switch on a transition. No WebCore change, no new IPC message,
no new serialized event field.

Why not gate on EventHandler::m_lastKnownMousePositionSource

LastKnownMousePositionSource (Source/WebCore/page/EventHandler.h:143, stored at :708, set at
EventHandler.cpp:5635) looks like it should already answer this, but it cannot:

  * Touch is reachable only through mousePositionSource(const PlatformMouseEvent&)
    (EventHandler.cpp:1983-1987), which returns Touch when syntheticClickType() != NoTap. The GTK
    port never sets a synthetic click type, so on GTK the value is never Touch. Its only reader is
    updateMouseEventTargetAfterLayoutIfNeeded() at EventHandler.cpp:3165, for a different purpose.

  * More decisively, a plain touch scroll on GTK4 delivers no mouse event at all. The touch-drag
    handlers synthesize only wheel events -- webkitWebViewBaseTouchDragBegin at
    Source/WebKit/UIProcess/API/gtk/WebKitWebViewBase.cpp:2236, TouchDragUpdate at :2263 and :2282,
    TouchSwipe at :2325. The mouse events those handlers do synthesize (already tagged
    PlatformMouseEvent::IsTouch::Yes at :2222-2224, :2260-2261, :2267, :2296) are emitted only for
    tap and long-press-drag, never for scrolling.

  * During a touch scroll the source therefore ends up Wheel, set either at EventHandler.cpp:3535
    or from WebPageProxy.cpp:4337 -- indistinguishable from a real mouse wheel. Making it
    distinguishable would mean a new field on PlatformWheelEvent and WebWheelEvent plus its
    serialization, touching every port, to answer a question the UI process already knows the
    answer to for free.

PlatformMouseEvent::IsTouch (Source/WebCore/platform/PlatformMouseEvent.h:91-93) is likewise no
help here: it is #if PLATFORM(GTK) only, has a single reader (DragController.cpp:1011), and again
is never set on the scroll path.

Correctness with both a touchscreen and a mouse

The flag is set true from exactly one place, webkitWebViewBaseTouchPress, whose GtkGestureClick is
gtk_gesture_single_set_touch_only(TRUE) (see webkitWebViewBaseConstructed), so it fires on finger
down and never for a pointer. Every touch interaction -- tap, drag-scroll, swipe, long press,
pinch -- begins with that press, and the handler has no early return.

It is set false from every real pointer entry point: the GtkEventControllerMotion handler
webkitWebViewBaseMotion, the GtkEventControllerScroll handler handleScroll (GTK4), and their GTK3
equivalents webkitWebViewBaseMotionNotifyEvent -- after its existing GDK_SOURCE_TOUCHSCREEN bail
out -- and webkitWebViewBaseScrollEvent. Touchscreens produce GDK_TOUCH_* events, not GDK_SCROLL
or GDK_MOTION_NOTIFY, so none of these fire for touch.

The two error directions are deliberately asymmetric. A missed "real pointer" only restores today's
behaviour, so the resets are placed early and liberally; a spurious "touch" would stop :hover
updating until the pointer moves, so the single setter is restricted to a touch-only gesture.
Hover on tap is unaffected: it is driven by the real Motion/Press/Release events synthesized in
webkitWebViewBaseTouchRelease, not by the fake-move timer. After a real wheel scroll with a mouse,
:hover still updates, because the wheel goes through handleScroll, which clears the flag first.

The transition guard means one IPC message per input-source change, not one per event -- zero
messages for the 314 scroll updates measured above.

Result on the target device: dispatchFakeMouseMoveEventSoon returns at EventHandler.cpp:3978 for
the whole drag, and the 75 hit tests, their forced layouts, and 437 ms of updateHoverActiveState
disappear.

